# Keep shell scripts LF so bash hooks run on every platform (CRLF breaks them).
*.sh text eol=lf
# bin/xrcat is a bash script without the .sh extension — same rule applies.
bin/xrcat text eol=lf
# x4-paths.env.example becomes a bash-sourced file when setup.sh copies it live;
# CRLF would put a trailing \r inside every value on Windows clones.
x4-paths.env.example text eol=lf
# Pin Python sources to LF (F53).
#
# WHY. `_freshness.hash_engine()` fingerprints artifacts by hashing the RAW BYTES of
# every file in `_freshness.ENGINE_SOURCES` -- EIGHT as of 2026-09-07: _cat.py,
# _diff.py, _effective.py, _loadorder.py, _merge.py, _registry.py, _scan.py and
# _xpath.py. Line endings are bytes.
# (2026-09-26: that roster is HISTORY. AUDIT-2026-09-24 FR-2 re-derived the list by
# tracing a store build: seven today -- _cat, _effective, _loadorder, _merge, _modfiles,
# _provenance, _registry. Derive it from the module; this count will rot too.)
#
# (This roster said SEVEN and omitted _loadorder.py until 2026-09-07 -- the same
# staleness aa1c67d fixed in CLAUDE.md, one file over, found by the round-2
# reviewer. Harmless here because `*.py text eol=lf` below covers every .py
# whatever this comment says; the roster is documentation, not the mechanism.
# Derive it from the module rather than retyping it -- that instruction has now
# been ignored twice, in two files, by people who had just read it.) With core.autocrlf=true and no rule here, a
# checkout writes CRLF while a tool that rewrites a file in place writes LF -- so the
# same commit hashes differently depending on how each file last arrived on disk.
#
# MEASURED 2026-08-25: this tree held three of the seven as CRLF (still as checked out)
# and four as LF (rewritten by tooling). Engine hash 73517fd69c0c1aad here versus
# 490e9a6a64909fa1 for `git archive HEAD` of the SAME commit, every file byte-identical
# after --strip-trailing-cr. Cost: a 26-gate sweep stopped mid-run and a wrong first
# diagnosis, because the staleness message asserts "the merge code has been edited"
# when nothing was.
#
# It fails safe -- a false STALE, never a false FRESH -- which is why it survived so
# long. The fix is to make the bytes deterministic rather than to trust habit.
# Shell scripts must be LF or Git Bash fails on every line with $'\r': command not found.
# Normalize everything else by default.
*.py text eol=lf
# Pin the SHIPPED game-side lua to LF, for the same reason and a second one.
#
# scripts/stamp-mod-build.py rewrites these two files in place with write_bytes,
# i.e. LF -- while `text=auto` alone lets a Windows clone check them out as CRLF.
# So the stamper's first run on a fresh Windows checkout rewrites every line of
# both files and produces a whole-file diff nobody asked for. Exactly F53, in the
# two files the *.py pin above does not cover.
#
# The BUILD hash itself is unaffected -- expected() reads through universal
# newlines, so it is stable across both endings -- which is precisely why this
# could sit here unnoticed: it shows up as noise in a diff, never as a wrong
# verdict.
mods/**/*.lua text eol=lf
# ...and its XML (F118). `* text=auto` alone checks these out with the platform's native
# ending, so a Windows clone got CRLF manifests while the deployed and committed copies are
# LF -- and `scripts/deploy-mod.py`, which compares raw bytes, then listed changes nobody
# made. The blobs are already LF, so this pin changes checkouts, never the repository.
mods/**/*.xml text eol=lf
# Normalize everything else by default.
* text=auto
# Maintainer release artifacts -- the Nexus upload packs.
#
# PUBLIC in the repo (nothing here is hidden; anyone can read them on GitHub) and
# deliberately NOT shipped inside a user's install. MEASURED 2026-09-09: without this,
# `git archive` put 17 release/nexus/ entries into the bundle -- rollback text,
# marketing bbcode and raw Nexus API responses -- growing it by 114,401 bytes for
# every installing user, none of whom has any use for them.
release/ export-ignore

# Maintainer-only: plants mutants in the game-side mod while it runs, so it must never
# reach a user. Tracked for maintainers; excluded from the bundle.
tools/x4validate/scripts/mutate-mod-lua.py export-ignore

# Maintainer working papers (v4.0.0 release review R6-12, lane FX-D). The implementation
# plans and session handoffs are PUBLIC in the repo but are not product documentation: they
# carry the maintainer's workspace details and per-lane process, and no installing user has
# any use for them. The measurements under docs/superpowers/measurements/ STAY in the bundle:
# the README and CHANGELOG cite them as evidence.
docs/superpowers/plans/ export-ignore
docs/handoffs/ export-ignore

# Maintainer-only: the isolated tool-audit harness (its README: "a local audit instrument,
# not a shipped CLI"). Tracked for maintainers; excluded from the bundle (v4.0.0 review R5-12).
tools/x4validate/audit/** export-ignore

# The vendored core's lock hashes checkout bytes, including its extensionless
# licence notice. Keep the same bytes in Windows, POSIX and archive installs.
tools/modding_harness/** text eol=lf
tools/modding-harness.lock.json text eol=lf
tools/x4_harness/** text eol=lf
agent/targets/opencode/*.js text eol=lf
# The source and runtime Codex templates enter reviewed candidate provenance.
agent/targets/codex/hooks.json.tmpl text eol=lf
tools/x4_harness/codex-hooks.json.tmpl text eol=lf
.opencode/plugins/x4guard.js text eol=lf
KNOWLEDGEBASE.local.md.example text eol=lf

# Codex limits the actual AGENTS.md bytes, including checkout newlines.
# A Windows CRLF checkout exceeded 32768 although the generated LF text fit.
/AGENTS.md text eol=lf
